Configuration Management = making many servers look the same.

Ansible helps you automate setup (install apps, create users, start services).

Ansible is agentless → connects using SSH.

Ansible uses YAML Playbooks to describe tasks.

Inventory file = list of servers.

Modules = actions (install package, copy file, start service).

Idempotent = running playbook again won’t break anything.

Handlers = run only when something changes.

Facts = basic system info Ansible collects.

Roles = organized way to reuse tasks.

Puppet needs agents; Ansible doesn’t.

Puppet = pull model; Ansible = push model.

Ansible is easier to learn for beginners.

Used for quick automation in DevOps projects.

Important Commands
ansible all -m ping -i inventory
ansible-playbook setup.yml -i inventory
ansible all -m yum -a "name=httpd state=present"
ansible-vault encrypt secrets.yml
ansible-galaxy init myrole

Real-World Usage

Set up 100 servers with the same apps in one click.

Automatically install and start services like Nginx, Docker, or MySQL.

Summary (4 lines)

Ansible automates server setup with simple YAML files.
It is agentless and works over SSH.
Puppet needs agents and is harder for beginners.
Ansible is faster and easier for DevOps teams to use daily.
